# A real Wii Remote is the Wii bridge's alone (issue #200, wiibridge/): no seat
# access grant on its raw node. With 99-cabinetos-wii-remote.rules.
SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", KERNELS=="0005:*", PROGRAM="/usr/bin/grep -q ^HID_NAME=Nintendo.RVL-CNT /sys%p/device/uevent", TAG-="uaccess"
# A REMOTE WITH NINTENDO'S OWN ID (Nintendo's, and copies that carry it) is
# taken by the kernel's Wii Remote driver too, hid-wiimote, which makes a
# controller of its own: SDL seated it as a second player beside W1, and every
# press counted twice (issue #548). The driver has to stay: the kernel keeps
# these ids for it (hid_have_special_driver), so without it the Remote gets no
# raw node and the bridge cannot reach it. Its devices are kept from everyone
# instead: no seat access, no joystick to anything that looks for one, and
# nothing for libinput (its D-pad is the arrow keys). These are the ids
# hid-wiimote takes, over Bluetooth only; the bridge's stand-ins are USB.
SUBSYSTEM=="input", KERNELS=="0005:057E:0306.*|0005:057E:0330.*", TAG-="uaccess", ENV{ID_INPUT_JOYSTICK}="", ENV{LIBINPUT_IGNORE_DEVICE}="1"
# The bridge makes its stand-ins through /dev/uhid. Bazzite grants it today, in
# ublue-os-udev-rules (60-sunshine-ublue.rules, for Sunshine); said here too, so
# real Wii Remotes do not depend on a streaming app's rule staying in the base.
KERNEL=="uhid", TAG+="uaccess"
